# G014 — Tax Calculator

*v2. See `tax-calculator.md` for prose: rules as a fifth resolver
primitive alongside computation, representation, scheduling, and
world-state lookup. Versioned rules differ from mutable facts —
rules change because humans decide, not because reality shifts.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Tax Planning Across Years

[tax-planning @client @current-year @next-year
    IN ORDER TO
        compare tax outcomes under the current and proposed rule sets
        for the client's projected income
        so the `where` reasons across rule versions, not just values

    @current-brackets <- [rules[tax-brackets {year: @current-year}]]
    @proposed-brackets <- [rules[tax-brackets {year: @next-year}]]

    @comparison <- [compare-outcomes
                      {income: @client:projected-income,
                       current: @current-brackets,
                       proposed: @proposed-brackets}]

    @comparison
        where proposed-liability is less than current-liability
            -> #deferral-beneficial
        otherwise
            -> #accelerate-income
]

---

## Verb Surface

[tax
    {income}                                 ;; -> current-year liability
    {income, year}
]

[tax-breakdown
    {income}                                 ;; -> [{bracket, rate, tax}]
]

[effective-rate
    {income}
]

[marginal-rate
    {income}
]

[rules
    tax-brackets        {year}               ;; -> [Bracket] versioned
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: rules versioned `where` year picks the policy

[rules[tax-brackets {year: @y}]
    IN ORDER TO
        fetch the bracket schedule enacted for tax year @y
        because tax law is versioned policy, not immutable computation
        and two different years legitimately return different schedules

    rule-store at (@y, "tax-brackets")
        where present -> return schedule
        otherwise      -> #rules-not-loaded {year: @y}
]

Fact lookup (G012) returns contingent world-state; rule lookup (G014)
returns intentional policy. Both bottom out in a store, but the
staleness semantics differ — facts drift, rules are legislated.
The resolver treats them as distinct primitives.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: apply brackets as progressive `where` ladder

[apply-brackets {income: @inc, brackets: @b}]
    total <- 0
    remaining <- @inc
    for @bracket in @b ordered by lower-bound ascending
        where remaining is at least bracket-width
            -> total += bracket-width × @bracket:rate
               remaining -= bracket-width
        where remaining is greater than zero
            -> total += remaining × @bracket:rate
               remaining := 0
    return total
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [tax {income: 50000, year: 2024}]             ;; -> e.g. 6053
    -> [tax-breakdown {income: 50000}]
    ;; -> [{bracket: "10%", amount: 1160}
    ;;     {bracket: "12%", amount: 4893}]

    -> [effective-rate {income: 50000}]               ;; -> 0.121
    -> [marginal-rate  {income: 50000}]               ;; -> 0.22
]
